DAY 19 – MODULES

1. WHAT ARE MODULES?

A module is a Python file containing code such as
functions, variables and classes.

Modules help organize Python programs into separate
and reusable files.


2. ADVANTAGES OF USING MODULES

• Code reusability
• Better code organization
• Reduces code duplication
• Easier maintenance
• Makes large programs easier to manage


3. TYPES OF MODULES

Python modules can be classified into:

• Built-in modules
• User-defined modules
• Third-party modules


4. IMPORTING MODULES

The import statement is used to use a module
in another Python program.

A module can be imported using its module name.


5. IMPORTING SPECIFIC FUNCTIONS

Specific functions or members can be imported
from a module.

This allows us to use only the required functionality
instead of importing the complete module.


6. IMPORTING MODULES USING ALIASES

An alias is an alternative name given to a module
while importing it.

Aliases make module names shorter and easier to use.


7. USER-DEFINED MODULES

A user-defined module is a Python file created
by the programmer.

It can contain functions, variables, classes
and other Python code.

User-defined modules help divide a large program
into smaller reusable files.


8. __NAME__ VARIABLE

__name__ is a special built-in variable in Python.

Its value depends on how the Python file is being used.

When a file is run directly, __name__ is set to
"__main__".


9. if __name__ == "__main__":

This condition is used to check whether a Python
file is being executed directly.

Code inside this block runs when the file is executed
directly and does not run in the same way when the
file is imported as a module.


10. BUILT-IN MODULES

Python provides many built-in modules.

Important modules covered include:

os → Operating system interaction

sys → System-specific functionality

platform → Information about the operating system
and platform

json → Working with JSON data

math → Mathematical operations

random → Random values and selections

collections → Specialized data structures

itertools → Tools for efficient iteration


KEY TAKEAWAY

Modules help in code reusability, better organization,
reduced code duplication and easier maintenance.

Python's built-in modules provide ready-made
functionality for different tasks such as system
interaction, mathematical operations, random values
and data structures.